WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:29.070 --> 00:00:30.760
Stanley Kubrick’s The Shining.

00:00:30.760 --> 00:00:35.050
Or, the world’s most persuasive argument
for taking the stairs.

00:00:35.050 --> 00:00:38.820
This movie scares the sh*t out of everyone.

00:00:38.820 --> 00:00:43.460
But why?

00:00:43.460 --> 00:00:49.340
It’s hard to say what exactly is wrong with
this place.

00:00:49.340 --> 00:00:50.340
Is it haunted?

00:00:50.340 --> 00:00:52.250
Is it all in their heads?

00:00:52.250 --> 00:00:54.180
Is it possessed by the devil?

00:00:54.180 --> 00:00:55.210
Tony?

00:00:55.210 --> 00:00:57.330
Something about an Indian burial ground?

00:00:57.330 --> 00:00:59.670
"You're located on an Indian burial ground."

00:00:59.670 --> 00:01:04.739
Whereas a lot of traditional horror gives
us an easy-to-understand reason to sh*t our

00:01:04.739 --> 00:01:09.580
pants, The Shining deliberately avoids an
easily describable premise - and this is part

00:01:09.580 --> 00:01:11.310
of what makes it so damn horrifying.

00:01:11.310 --> 00:01:12.310
"You like it?"

00:01:12.310 --> 00:01:14.990
It deprives us of logical explanation.

00:01:14.990 --> 00:01:20.290
Kubrick accomplishes this in a variety of
ways, but I’m here at the Overlook Hotel,

00:01:20.290 --> 00:01:23.030
to talk about one of the most surprisingly
simple: “perspective”.

00:01:23.030 --> 00:01:28.710
One of the most effective ways to create a
strong sense of perspective is through the

00:01:28.710 --> 00:01:31.270
way a shot is framed and sequenced.

00:01:31.270 --> 00:01:37.000
See, a shot can be objective, subjective,
or fall somewhere in between.

00:01:37.000 --> 00:01:42.190
Purely objective shots show us a scene “as
it is,” a God’s-eye point of view, as

00:01:42.190 --> 00:01:45.549
if we’re eavesdropping or watching a play.

00:01:45.549 --> 00:01:50.220
Subjective angles directly insert us into
the action, showing the view from, just beside,

00:01:50.220 --> 00:01:52.579
or behind a character.

00:01:52.579 --> 00:01:57.759
Purely subjective shots take us directly into
the eyes of a character, usually by showing

00:01:57.759 --> 00:02:02.090
their face first, followed by their visual
perspective or emotional experience.

00:02:02.090 --> 00:02:08.759
It can also invite us to question what we’re
seeing, like an unreliable narrator in literature.

00:02:08.759 --> 00:02:13.459
In this scene, we see a shot of Danny’s
face, and then a cut to the girls, then Danny’s

00:02:13.459 --> 00:02:14.600
face again.

00:02:14.600 --> 00:02:19.250
With this simple sequence of shots, we are
being shown the events through Danny’s mind.

00:02:19.250 --> 00:02:24.239
Let’s see how Kubrick takes this basic principle
of perspective to create an indescribable

00:02:24.239 --> 00:02:29.420
supernatural feeling.

00:02:29.420 --> 00:02:32.870
Consider the famous scene of Danny riding
his tricycle through the hotel’s winding

00:02:32.870 --> 00:02:33.870
corridors.

00:02:33.870 --> 00:02:36.140
"Alright, Danny, gimme some skin!

00:02:36.140 --> 00:02:37.140
...Alright."

00:02:37.140 --> 00:02:41.199
Using a state of the art piece of equipment
for the time, Kubrick is able to achieve supernaturally

00:02:41.199 --> 00:02:46.550
smooth movements to follow Danny, showing
us the hotel from a child’s point of view.

00:02:46.550 --> 00:02:51.250
We follow Danny in a traditional subjective
shot that emphasizes his experience navigating

00:02:51.250 --> 00:02:57.760
a large maze-like hotel, but we don’t follow
him perfectly - watch how the camera lingers

00:02:57.760 --> 00:03:02.390
after he’s left frame… and then hurries
to catch up with him.

00:03:02.390 --> 00:03:06.579
One film scholar calls this “the wandering
camera,” because the camera ambles along

00:03:06.579 --> 00:03:13.060
like its own sentient being, or here, an imagined
monster stalking Danny through the halls.

00:03:13.060 --> 00:03:18.820
This creates the sense the hotel is alive
and watching Danny throughout his sh*tty vacation.

00:03:18.820 --> 00:03:20.989
When the twins show up, — "Come play with
us."

00:03:20.989 --> 00:03:25.949
— we move more intimately into a subjective
sequence of Danny’s POV, flashing between

00:03:25.949 --> 00:03:27.320
his bloody visions.

00:03:27.320 --> 00:03:31.090
Danny covers his eyes, and looks again...

00:03:31.090 --> 00:03:32.370
And now they’re gone.

00:03:32.370 --> 00:03:35.639
So, perhaps the girls are just in his head?

00:03:35.639 --> 00:03:39.979
By starting with Danny’s point of view as
we follow him, but then shifting to to a possible

00:03:39.979 --> 00:03:45.750
camera-monster’s point of view, and then
back to Danny’s maybe unreliable own vision,

00:03:45.750 --> 00:03:50.090
the film undermines our ability to interpret
the events in front of us: whose point of

00:03:50.090 --> 00:03:51.430
view is this?

00:03:51.430 --> 00:03:52.430
Danny’s?

00:03:52.430 --> 00:03:53.430
The Hotel?

00:03:53.430 --> 00:03:54.430
Some Demon?

00:03:54.430 --> 00:03:57.290
Now let’s consider Jack.

00:03:57.290 --> 00:04:00.060
Is all of of this creepy stuff in his head,
too?.

00:04:00.060 --> 00:04:05.440
Did someone just spike the water with LSD
in this otherwise normal hotel?.

00:04:05.440 --> 00:04:10.280
Take this scene in the apparently empty Gold
Ballroom.

00:04:10.280 --> 00:04:14.630
In this scene, we go from a close-up of Jack,
as he seemingly addresses an invisible being

00:04:14.630 --> 00:04:15.630
— "Hi, Lloyd."

00:04:15.630 --> 00:04:17.980
— to the reveal shot of Lloyd.

00:04:17.980 --> 00:04:22.590
This subjective sequencing of shots could
suggest that Jack is hallucinating.

00:04:22.590 --> 00:04:27.460
And just when we’re sure that this is all
a subjective delusion, Kubrick moves the camera

00:04:27.460 --> 00:04:33.000
backward over his shoulder, taking the shot
from his direct point of view to a more objective

00:04:33.000 --> 00:04:34.280
one.

00:04:34.280 --> 00:04:38.760
From there, Kubrick gives the scene an objective
reality by showing both characters in a shot

00:04:38.760 --> 00:04:39.760
together.

00:04:39.760 --> 00:04:42.430
Lloyd’s the real deal… we think.

00:04:42.430 --> 00:04:46.760
Until Wendy shows up, and the ballroom’s
suddenly empty, and we’re left totally confused

00:04:46.760 --> 00:04:47.760
and creeped out.

00:04:47.760 --> 00:04:49.890
"Are you out of your f**king mind?"

00:04:49.890 --> 00:04:51.560
So, what’s going on?

00:04:51.560 --> 00:04:55.900
We don’t know, and the camera work makes
sure we’ll never find out.

00:04:55.900 --> 00:05:01.400
Now, at times, Jack’s point of view even
seems to merge with that of the imagined predator

00:05:01.400 --> 00:05:04.410
that was following Danny.

00:05:04.410 --> 00:05:08.810
In this shot, Jack’s view looking down on
the model hedge maze blends into a aerial

00:05:08.810 --> 00:05:12.500
view of his family wandering through the actual
hedges.

00:05:12.500 --> 00:05:16.190
This effect happens again as he chases Danny
in the final scene.

00:05:16.190 --> 00:05:19.820
See how the same kind of shot that used to
be stalking Danny through the halls has now

00:05:19.820 --> 00:05:24.710
mingled with Jack’s point of view, further
confounding the various supernatural elements

00:05:24.710 --> 00:05:27.080
of the film.

00:05:27.080 --> 00:05:31.280
When not using point of view to screw with
reality, Kubrick also uses our perception

00:05:31.280 --> 00:05:34.780
of physical space to creep us out.

00:05:34.780 --> 00:05:40.960
How do you make a huge, otherwise normal hotel
and present it as a claustrophobic nightmare?

00:05:40.960 --> 00:05:46.680
Kubrick is famous for what is called one-point
perspective, which adds to the cramped feel.

00:05:46.680 --> 00:05:52.060
With “one point perspective”, near-perfect
symmetry draws the audience's eye to one vanishing

00:05:52.060 --> 00:05:56.310
point at the center of the frame, giving the
sensation that we’re being drawn into the

00:05:56.310 --> 00:05:59.980
shot, which is further enhanced by the tracking
shot inward.

00:05:59.980 --> 00:06:03.320
It feels uneasy, like the walls are collapsing
inwards.

00:06:03.320 --> 00:06:08.880
That’s why, even though the Overlook Hotel
seems like a spacious setting, we feel increasingly

00:06:08.880 --> 00:06:13.470
cramped as the movie goes on, almost like:
"A kind of claustrophobic reaction, which

00:06:13.470 --> 00:06:17.960
can occur when people are shut in together
over long periods of time."

00:06:17.960 --> 00:06:23.520
By juxtaposing large spaces with much smaller
ones, Kubrick is able to get the viewer to

00:06:23.520 --> 00:06:25.730
really feel claustrophobic.

00:06:25.730 --> 00:06:30.480
This starts with the opening shot of the mountain,
which cuts to the shot of the family, clown-car

00:06:30.480 --> 00:06:31.480
style.

00:06:31.480 --> 00:06:36.230
Then we move from the large sweeping hotel
lobby to the Torrences’ small hotel quarters.

00:06:36.230 --> 00:06:41.760
While the hotel may be large, the Torrences
are trapped in increasingly smaller spaces

00:06:41.760 --> 00:06:42.760
within it.

00:06:42.760 --> 00:06:44.720
"Well, it's very... uh... homey!"

00:06:44.720 --> 00:06:50.990
Eventually, Jack ends up locked in a fridge,
Danny escapes through a tiny window, and nobody

00:06:50.990 --> 00:06:53.040
has any elbow room.

00:06:53.040 --> 00:06:58.160
Through these tricks in perspective, Stanley
Kubrick is able to add a layer of creepiness

00:06:58.160 --> 00:07:04.310
that evades simple description - and that’s
why The Shining is so great, and why film

00:07:04.310 --> 00:07:07.389
nerds will continue to talk about it for years
to come.

00:07:07.389 --> 00:07:11.770
There is so much more to talk about in The
Shining, so if you liked this video, let us

00:07:11.770 --> 00:07:30.050
know if you want 
us

00:07:30.050 --> 00:07:37.419
to do another.

00:07:37.419 --> 00:08:14.650
And as always, thanks

00:08:14.650 --> 00:08:21.460
for watching.

00:08:21.460 --> 00:08:28.130
Peace.

